# TypeScript Guide

Strict mode (`tsconfig.json`). Naming and exports: `rules/general-coding-principles.mdc`.

## Types
- `interface` for object shapes, component props, and class contracts; `type` for unions, intersections, mapped types, tuples, and function signatures. No `I` prefix on new interfaces.
- Model mutually exclusive state as a discriminated union, not optional fields that can drift out of sync. Switch on it with an exhaustiveness check (`assertNever`), so a new member fails to compile until handled.
- Derive types instead of duplicating them by hand (`Pick`, `Omit`, `Partial`, `Required`, `Readonly`, `Record`, `ReturnType`, `Parameters`); validate a literal with `satisfies` without widening it. Derive a union from its runtime `as const` object.

## Strictness — don't weaken it
- Never `any`. Use `unknown` and narrow at the boundary. A server entity is parsed with its zod schema (`rules/api-contract.mdc`), not a handwritten `typeof` guard; a type guard is for a value that has no schema.
- No unchecked `as Foo` cast to silence errors — fix the type or narrow safely. `as const` is fine; `as unknown as Foo` is not.
- No `// @ts-ignore` / `// @ts-expect-error` to hide real errors, and no non-null assertion (`!`) to bypass a null check — guard instead.

## Functions and components
- Exported functions and components have explicit return types; inference does not leak into the public API.
- Props are an `interface` declared in the component file, above the component, once there is more than one prop — never in `types.ts`. Other types go in `types.ts`: component-local ones in the component folder, domain ones in `features/*/models/types.ts` / `entities/*/model/types.ts`. No `types.ts` when the props interface was the only type. A `cva` `VariantProps` type stays in `styles.ts`. Nested components and helpers: `rules/component-structure.mdc`.
- A single options object instead of 3+ positional parameters.
- Name a generic for what it represents (`TData`, `TError`) unless a lone `T` is obvious. Constrain it (`<T extends object>`) when the body relies on shape.

## Closed sets
- A `const` object plus a derived union — no TypeScript `enum`, no `src/enums/`.
- The const and every map of its values (label keys, error codes, predicates, the type guard) live in one file — no `labels.ts` or `status-map.ts`. When any map holds a translation key, that file is the slice `config/`, so `models/` does not import keys; `models/` may `import type` the value union.
- A key map is `satisfies Record<Union, string>` and holds translation keys, never English copy (`rules/i18n.mdc`). `ui/` calls `t(map[value])` and declares no other map.

